@broadcast "Jackpot Protocol"
@channel KSAB
@category entertainment
@type gameshow
@subject basin
@host npc_jp_thorne
@sidekick npc_jp_okoye
@titlecard jackpot_protocol_logo
@length 600
@airtime 19
@rounds 4

# Jackpot Protocol used to be a scripted tape: three strangers with fixed names answered
# fixed questions and a fixed one of them won 250,000 credits, every night, forever. The
# prize alone gave it away — the dearest object in the Basin is worth nine thousand.
#
# It is now a live quiz. The questions are the city itself: the districts described in
# their own authored copy, the four orders quoted from their own creeds, and where the
# people you pass in the street actually go home to. None of that is written twice — it
# is the world's own text read back at you, which means a district added next month
# becomes a question with no authoring at all.
#
# Anyone standing in the KSAB soundstage when a round opens is a contestant, and their
# answer goes out to every set in the Basin. The three strangers are still here for the
# nights nobody comes.
#
# Marisa Thorne believes this is a serious civic institution. Bertrand Okoye has read the
# ratings.

::actors
@actor npc_jp_thorne
@alias npc_jp_thorne THORNE
@actor npc_jp_okoye
@alias npc_jp_okoye OKOYE
::endactors

::contestants
Pell Anselm
Nine-of-Cups
Dorothy Vane
A Caller Who Declined To Be Named
Sunder
Ma Rethick
The Gentleman From Ashway
::endcontestants

# ── Cold open ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines open
From the KSAB soundstage, under lights that cost more than the prize — this is JACKPOT PROTOCOL.
Good evening, Coldwater Basin. Tonight we find out how much of your own city you were actually looking at.
This is the programme where knowing where you live is worth money. Briefly. Once.
Nine districts, four orders, and a hundred and eighty people who all sleep somewhere. Let's see who's been paying attention.
::endlines

::lines announce_host
The only woman in the Basin who reads the census for pleasure — {host}!
She has never once been to Ashway and can describe it to you in full — {host}!
Ladies, gentlemen, and whatever the Synthesis are calling it this week — {host}!
::endlines

::lines audience_call
If you're standing on my floor, you're playing. Type {verb} and a letter. That's the whole apparatus.
Anyone in this studio may answer — {verb}, then A, B, C or D. Say it on television and mean it.
You. Yes. {verb} and a letter. You have lived here your whole life. Prove some of it.
The rules have not changed since the format changed: I ask, you {verb} a letter, the Basin watches you be wrong.
::endlines

# ── Round intros ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The generic key does the work — every round of this show is the same shape, which is
# the point. The question and the options are read by `prompt`.
#
# NO ORDINALS IN HERE. This pool is drawn from at random, once per round, so a line
# saying "round three" will air first about as often as it airs third. The Last Lot gets
# away with ordinals because its pools are per-FORMAT and its formats are per-round;
# this show has one format all the way down. Say "next" and "again", never "third".
::lines round_intro.choice
Here we go. A question, some answers, and about forty seconds of you looking at the ceiling.
Next question. One of these is true and the rest were written to look like it.
On we go. Statistically, two of you are about to pick the same wrong one.
Same shape, harder ground. Let's see.
Another one. The field is wider than it looks and so is the silence.
And again. Nobody has ever made this look as difficult as you're all about to.
::endlines

::lines showcase_intro
And now the money. THE JACKPOT ROUND. Four answers, one question, and a purse that would clear most people's rent.
Our last question of the night. This is the one they'll talk about on the platform tomorrow. Four options.
The Jackpot Round. Bertrand has the card. I have the question. You have about a minute.
::endlines

# ── The announcer's read ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# {prize} is the CATEGORY here rather than an object — a quiz has no lot on a plinth.
::lines prize_copy
Tonight's category: {prize}.
The category is {prize}. Play it as it lies.
{prize}. Standard rules, standard purse, no appeals.
This one comes to us from {prize}, and the network has checked it twice.
{prize}. Answer with a letter and try to look confident about it.
::endlines

# ── The question ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The prompt IS the question — {question} then {options}. This is the guess window.
::lines prompt
{question} Your options: {options}.
Here it is. {question} {options}.
{question} And the answers — {options}.
Listen carefully. {question} Options: {options}.
::endlines

::lines stall
Take your time. The city took two hundred years to get like this.
I can hear somebody in the third row saying it out loud. Don't help them. They're wrong.
Bertrand has the sealed card and the expression of a man who already knows.
Answer, somebody. The lights are the only thing in this building that runs on schedule.
Anyone. A letter. It costs you nothing and it might cost you everything.
Still time. Not much of it.
::endlines

# ── The reveal ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines reveal
Answers in — {guesses}. And the correct answer is {answer}.
The floor said {guesses}. The card says {answer}.
{guesses}. And it was {answer} all along.
Let's have it. {guesses}. Bertrand — the card. It's {answer}.
The floor gave me {guesses}. The Basin gives me {answer}.
::endlines

::lines showcase_reveal
The Jackpot answers: {guesses}. And the card says {answer}.
{guesses}, for {purse} credits. And the correct answer — {answer}.
Sealed card, Bertrand. Thank you. It's {answer}. The floor said {guesses}.
::endlines

::lines verdict_read
{verdict} That's {purse} credits and a place in the transcript.
{verdict} Take the money. It clears before the network notices.
{verdict} And that, ladies and gentlemen, is what knowing your own street is worth.
{verdict} Bertrand, mark it down. Mark it in ink, we're not the shopping channel.
{verdict} The Basin thanks you for your civic attention.
::endlines

# ── Crowd ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
::lines audience
The audience makes a noise somewhere between a cheer and an apology.
Somebody says the right letter out loud, half a second too late, to nobody.
A groan travels the length of the floor and expires near the exit.
The applause comes in unevenly, the way it does when nobody is sure yet.
Two rows of people turn to each other and disagree, quietly, on television.
Somewhere in the dark a chair goes over.
::endlines

::lines applause
♪ Applause, big and slightly too long, the way the floor manager likes it. ♪
♪ The audience comes in hard on the sting. ♪
♪ Applause, and the theme underneath it, and the lights going up. ♪
::endlines

::lines commercial
KSAB will return to JACKPOT PROTOCOL. Stay where you are. The lights are on.
A bumper plays: the logo, spinning, over a city that does not look like this one.
KSAB. We are legally required to tell you the prize money is real.
::endlines

::lines walkoff
{contestant} has left the studio floor and forfeits the answer.
::endlines

::lines no_payout
The network says one purse a day. Bertrand did not write that rule and neither did I.
::endlines

::lines signoff
That's our programme. Go home. You clearly need the practice.
From all of us at JACKPOT PROTOCOL — know your city. It certainly knows you.
Goodnight, Coldwater Basin. Look up on the way home.
::endlines

::lines ticker
JACKPOT PROTOCOL is recorded before a live studio audience · Prize money subject to network verification · Contestant answers are broadcast in full and cannot be withdrawn · KSAB Entertainment
::endlines
